														In response to the note from Aam: While I may not be a very active Guild member, I enjoy very much the interaction and the play of the game and guild. However some comments that were made about the officers not participating or contributing is not entirely accurate, as the example of the cancelled quests. The guild motd popped for over a week to contact the 2 characters in game for info or to hit the website and a few in guild chat even asked about it in general. As a member in knowing of the raids/quests i responded to them to talk to the appropriate people. Very few if any one responded and the raid was therefore cancelled. The day AFTER the raid was cancelled everybody asked about it. This shows a lack of interest and partiocipation in the players instead of the officers in charge. The website was offered to be designed, built and hosted on one of the officers' servers, and that offer was declined. I know this response may not seem in good taste from one who is not as active as the others, but it is a game and meant to be fun and enjoyable. I have noticed that it is usually the same dozen or so characters speaking to other guild members in /guild all the time and that even to the casual observer shows a genuine lack of interest in guild sponsored events. As a RL manager, I know the frustration of leading where only a few want to follow and can see Aam's frustration, but based on my observations, the blame of the officers is misplaced. Also, since the message was posted, the guild has thinned dramatically. This is also a sign of fickleness in members. If the guild disbands, then so be it as this is a part of EQ life, but it is also very sad that it happens all too often and a game that is built as an escape from RL has so much of the stress of RL filtering through. If the guild does not disband, it will soon if members keep dumping off, and we will all be looking for another group of acceptance towards all of us.						</td>
